  baseline / target / guardrail stay `null` until measured; never guess them.
-->

## Sources

Where the review looks. One bullet per source: what it is, how to reach it read-only, and what it is authoritative for. Mark any source that needs credentials the review may not have — an unreachable source becomes a recorded gap, not a failed run.

- TODO

## Metric queries

The exact read-only queries behind the KPI and the supporting counts. Give each a name the report can cite, the query text, and the window it covers.

- TODO

## Log queries

The read-only searches that surface failures for classification. Name each one, state its window, and note the volume to expect so a suspiciously empty result reads as a gap rather than as health.

- TODO

## Failure-class taxonomy

The closed set of classes every observed failure is sorted into. One subsection per class: what it looks like, what distinguishes it from its nearest neighbour, and any caution that has previously caused a misclassification. An `unclassified` bucket is mandatory — a class that does not exist yet must surface as unclassified, never get forced into the closest match.

- TODO

## Fix verification checks

Per entry in `verify_tickets`: what shipped, how to tell from read-only evidence whether it is actually deployed, and what observable signal proves it worked. A check with no observable signal is not a check — say so rather than inventing one.

- TODO

## Monitor publication gates

Per entry in `monitors`: what the monitor would watch and the gate — the condition that must hold before a human is asked to publish it. Reviews evaluate the gate and recommend; they never publish, enable, silence, or edit a monitor.

- TODO

## Forecast heuristics

How to project the next 24–72 hours from what the review just measured: which quantities extrapolate, which do not, known cyclicality, and the conditions under which the forecast should be withheld instead of guessed.

- TODO

## Safety rails

Protocol-specific limits on top of the domain's read-only default: queries that are expensive enough to need a narrower window, fields that must be redacted before they reach a report, and anything a review must refuse outright.

